              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#CohomotopyInTopologicalDataAnalysisReferences"} Cohomotopy in topological data analysis
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#further_variants"} Further variants
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            %em Persistent homology
            is the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 2
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \\xrightarrow{\;}
                  V_i
                  \\xrightarrow{\phi_i}
                  V_{i + 1}
                  \\xrightarrow{\phi_{i + 1}}
                  V_{i + 2}
                  \\xrightarrow{\phi_{i + 2}}
                  \\cdots
                  \\,,
          %p
            under the aspect of which elements (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"}> vectors
            )
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_i \in V_i
            “persist” (ie. remain non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"}> zero
            ) under which number of iterations of these linear maps. One then refers to these sequences as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+modules"} persistence modules
            (hence a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concept+with+an+attitude"} concept with an attitude
          %p
            In the archetypical applications of interest, these vector spaces are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_i \,=\, H_n(X_i)
            (over a given
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
            which themselves are stages
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ι
                        %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ι
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots \hookrightarrow X_i \xhookrightarrow{\iota_i} X_{i+ 1} \xhookrightarrow{\iota_{i + 1}} X_{i + 2} \xhookrightarrow{} \cdots \xhookrightarrow{x} X
          %p
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+topological+space"} filtered topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
            which stand out as persisting over a large range of steps.
          %p
            This question, in turn, has been motivated from problems in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+data+analysis"} topological data analysis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
            arise as coarse-grained versions, at varying stages of resolution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            (say as measured in some ambient
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete set
            of data points. In this example, the more the homology cycles
            %em persist
            as the resolution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is varied, the more one is willing to assume that they signal relevant structure that is hidden in the data, while cycles that do not persist for long would be interpreted as irrelevant noise effects.
          %p Of course, these simple basic ideas may be (and still are being) refined and generalized in a number of ways.
          %p
            A particularly important generalization turns out to be that from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} directed 1-dimensional
            sequences of linear maps to arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzags"} zigzags
            of these, then called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+persistence+modules"} zigzag persistence modules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+topological+space"} filtered topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospans"} cospans
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ←
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ←
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo ←
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots \leftarrow X_i \to X_i \cup X_{i + 1} \leftarrow X_{i + 1} \to X_{i + 1} \cup X_{i + 2} \leftarrow \cdots
          %p
            of inclusions into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            of consecutive filter stages.
          %p
            Seen in this generality, the notion of finite-length
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+persistence+modules"} zigzag persistence modules
            happens to coincide with that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} A-type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representations"} quiver representations
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            to bear on persistent homology theory.
          %p
            In particular, the founding result of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representation+theory"} quiver representation theory
            – namely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel%27s+theorem"} Gabriel's theorem
            – serves, in hindsight, to establish the formal notion of persistence itself: The theorem implies (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Gabriel's+theorem#ATypeQuivers"} there
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+persistence"} zigzag
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+module"} persistence module
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            %em interval modules
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_{a,b}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
            except over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in [a,b]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            (the latter regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            over itself).
          %p
            Hence the canonical
            = succeed "-elements" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_{a,b}
            are exactly the persistent cycles which
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                persist from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel%27s+theorem"} Gabriel's theorem
            guarantees that any (zigzag) persistence module may be completely decomposed into (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+span"} linear span
            of) these elements. Numerous generalizations of this theorem exist (taking it out of the context of
            = succeed "-theory)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            to be taken from sets with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
          %p
            In other words, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+class"} isomorphism class
            of any (zigzag) persistence module is equivalently encoded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiset"} multiset
            of intervals. These multisets are known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/barcodes"} barcodes
            (due to the evident graphical representation of a multiset of intervals) or as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagrams"} persistence diagrams
            (the latter usually when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b]
            is regarded as a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \in \mathbb{R}^2
            in the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagrams"} persistence diagrams
            are meant to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariants"} invariants
            of interest in persistent homology theory.
          %p
            Besides the foundational theorem that guarantees the existence of persistence diagrams, the fundamental theorem of persistent homology has come to be the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+of+persistence+diagrams"} stability theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagrams"} persistence diagrams
            are indeed
            = succeed "," do
              %em useful invariants
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
          %p
            For example, as one changes a little the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            with which to produce the topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
            of coarse grained data at resolution stage
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X_i)
            may change dramatically, but the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+of+persistence+diagrams"} stability theorem
            ensures that effect on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagram"} persistence diagram
            remains small.
          %p
            It is in this way that persistent homology may be used, in particular, to produce robust invariants of noisy data, which has made it the archetypical tool in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+data+analysis"} topological data analysis
